  "query_preview": "..\n(Thursday through next Monday)\nIssued at 200 PM CDT Tue Jul 21 2026\n\nThe heat gets cranked up a little more on Thursday and and heat \nadvisory will likely be required for much of the Panhandles. The \nhottest readings are expected across the north-central Panhandles \nfrom just north of Amarillo to Borger, Dumas, Stratford, Perryton\nand Guymon and there is a 40-80% probability of seeing highs \ngreater than 104 in those areas. The other area of higher \ntemperatures with similar probabilities is in PDC and ESE from \nthere to Wellington and south from there.\n\nThere have been some subtle changes in models handling of the H5 \nhigh center late this week that have implications of the forecast \nstarting Thursday night. GFS, ECMWF, GEM and NAM all track the H5\ncenter a bit further south as it retrogrades west and they agree\non a little more aggressive west movement than indicated a few \ndays ago. This isn't going to save us from the heat, but it opens \nthe door for dirty ridge roller s/wvs to work from CO ESE across \nthe region late Thu and Fri. Given this and strong H7 theta-e \nridging associated with monsoon plume, the southern edge of \nscattered storms moving out of CO into KS are expected to impact \nthe far NW Panhandles Thu evening. There actually is some modest \nwind shear noted in models, but mid level temperatures are \nextremely warm (-2 to -3 deg C). This will limit CAPE, but \ncertainly not DCAPE which will be very high. This suggests strong \nwind gusts, lightning and heavy downpours are the thunderstorms \nhazards of concern with this activity.\n\n6Z models started a trend of bringing the hybrid outflow/frontal \nboundary associated with this activity much further south and \ninitiate storms along that boundary anywhere from the central \nPanhandles to the far southern Panhandles late Friday afternoon. \nThis of course will bring uncertainty to temperature forecast as \nwell on Friday, but it will get plenty hot before storms fire late\nin the afternoon. For not POPs are limited to 20 percent mainly \ndue to uncertainties wrt boundary location, but these may increase\ninto the SCT category once boundary position is better known.\n\nAfter retrograding west, the high is forecast to build east again \nover the NRN Panhandles and/or SW KS over the weekend. This suggest \nmore heat and less storm chances heading into next week.",
